A||||6||RICK||2.8 rebuild
Z||000000||RICK||07-15-99||07:54 AM||||I'm starting to rebuild my 2.8 buy I don't want to be without my Fiero.  My frind has a salvage yard and said that he would give me a 2.8 block to do the rebuild on so I could just swap the engine out when it came time.  My question is, are all 2.8 Chevy/Pontiac blocks the same for the 84-88 (bolt holes, freeze plugs, water pumps, ect...)?
Z||000001||PaUL||07-15-99||09:09 AM||||Rick, the 2.8s made before 85 have a smaller main journal diameter on the crank. Other then that there are pretty much the same. If you use an older block from a carbed car, youll have to plug the hole for the mechanical fuel pump on the side of the block. 
Z||000002||PaUL||07-15-99||09:11 AM||||I should also mention that the valves on the fiero 2.8 are bigger than most 2.8s used on other cars. Its the HO version of the 2.8. But the heads are exactly the same other then that. 
Z||000003||batboy||07-15-99||04:58 PM||||PaUL is right. All 1985 and later 2.8's with fuel injection had the bigger crank journal because they found out the smaller ones were a little weak (a few S-10's with carbs still used the older cranks). Use the HO heads off your Fiero or find another pair, those bigger valves are worth a few extra horsepower. If you found a 3.1 short block to rebuild and used all of your Fiero's external stuff (including the heads) that might work out pretty good.
Z||000004||RICK||07-15-99||05:07 PM||||I'm going to be using the existing heads because they are the HO, when I was talking to my friend at the salvage yard his computer was saying that the crank is different for the 87, 88 and the timming cover???  I don't want to get the engine bored out, cross drilled, and everything else and find out something will not bolt up.<br>Thanks for any advise
Z||000005||PaUL||07-15-99||07:30 PM||||The timing cover may be different but the one from your fiero should bolt onto any 2.8 block. I think on the older engines the cover sticks out more.
Z||000006||GREENDANO||07-15-99||09:41 PM||||The timing cover difference relates to what kind of oil pan it has.  The 87's and 88's have a one piece oil pan gasket that seals much better.  If you can get complete matched set, it's the prefered gasket because it doesn't leak.  Only one thing, Fieros were the only car that had that cover and pan with the one piece seal and v-belt water pump.  Other 2.8/3.1 in different chassis' are close but they use a serpentine belt which means it also has a different water pump.  The 87-88  crank is different because it is internally balanced.  So if you get the newer block, you need the matching flywheel/flexplate and also the correct balancer.  A complete '87 or'88 'Fiero' V6 is a "Nice find" in the junkyard for sure!!<br>  Any iron cylinder head from a fuel injected  60 degree V6 will work 2.8/3.1/3.4. All EFI heads are HO (big valve heads) The Hollender interchange doesn't list that it's the same, but it is.  I have a '89 S-10 head on my '85 GT and have no problems.<br>  Dan-O
